<description>
Fill each hollow blue rectangular frame with red for even interior area and orange for odd interior area.

Input structure
- A black background contains one or more one-cell-thick blue rectangular frames.
- Each frame encloses a black rectangular interior.
- Frames may be separate or touch along their outside borders.

Rule
- Detect every blue rectangular border in the original input.
- Compute each frame's interior area.
- Fill the interior red when that area is even, or orange when it is odd.
- Leave the blue borders and all exterior background unchanged.

Key insight
- The fill color encodes the parity of the empty rectangular interior, so
  frames must be detected as rectangles rather than foreground components.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "one-cell-thick blue rectangular frames on a black background, each enclosing black interior pixels; frames may touch externally",
  "transform": "fill each frame interior red if its interior area is even and orange if it is odd; preserve frame borders and exterior background"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10-20 rows x 10-20 cols, always square [observed]
  background: black (0)
  frames:
    count: 2-5 per grid
    shape: always square, 1-cell-thick blue (1) hollow rectangles
    outer_side: 3-10 cells (interior side = outer_side - 2, so 1-8)
    interior: black in input; filled in output
    non_overlapping: frame bounding boxes never overlap; min 1-cell gap between any two frame borders
    parity_mix: each example has at least one even-area and one odd-area interior [observed]
  transform:
    interior_area = (outer_side - 2)^2
    fill red (2) if interior_area is even, orange (7) if odd
    blue borders and exterior background unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (input is the constrained structure; output is a simple derivation)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid side N in [10, 20].
    2. Pick number of frames K in [2, 5].
    3. For each frame, pick outer side length S in [3, 10].
    4. Place frames on grid with no bounding-box overlap and >=1-cell gap between borders. Use random placement with rejection or greedy packing.
    5. Ensure at least one frame has even interior area and one has odd. Interior area = (S-2)^2; even when S is even, odd when S is odd. If all same parity, swap one frame's S.
    6. Draw each frame as a 1-cell-thick blue border with black interior on a black grid.
    7. Output = input with each interior filled red (even area) or orange (odd area).
```
